      "id": 1,
      "text": "Redbird Smith, Oklahoma, is named for the traditionalist Cherokee leader who founded the Keetoowah Nighthawk Society around 1900.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redbird_Smith%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Redbird Smith established a sacred ceremonial ground here for rituals and stomp dances to preserve Cherokee cultural practices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redbird_Smith%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community emerged as a hub for a resistance movement that opposed U.S. land allotments under the Dawes Act.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/redbird_smith_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Redbird Smith, Oklahoma, is located in the eastern hills near Blackgum Mountain within historic Cherokee territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
